CVE-2025-61725

# Go stdlib crypto/x509 in rclone / lazygit: excluded subdomain constraint
# bypass in cert chain validation. CLI tools in sandboxed container, not exposed
# to untrusted certificate chains. Fix: Go 1.24.11 / 1.25.5.
CVE-2025-61727

# perl-modules-5.36 (Debian bookworm): Archive::Tar extracts symlinks with
# attacker-controlled targets outside the extraction directory (symlink traversal
# in _make_special_file). Impact: NONE - container does not extract untrusted tar
# archives; perl is invoked only by codeflare-managed entrypoint scripts and
# system package tooling. No fix available in Debian bookworm (requires Perl 5.38+
# with Archive::Tar 3.08).
CVE-2026-42496

# perl-modules-5.36 / libperl5.36 (Debian bookworm): Archive::Tar before 3.08
# extracts hardlinks with attacker-controlled targets, allowing files outside
# the extraction directory to be linked (hardlink traversal). Impact: NONE -
# container does not extract untrusted tar archives; perl is invoked only by
# codeflare-managed entrypoint scripts and system package tooling. Same exposure
# profile as CVE-2026-42496 above. No fix available in Debian bookworm (requires
# Perl 5.38+ with Archive::Tar 3.08).
CVE-2026-42497

# perl-modules-5.36 / libperl5.36 (Debian bookworm): Archive::Tar before 3.10
# allows memory exhaustion via crafted tar archives. Impact: NONE - container
# does not extract untrusted tar archives; perl is invoked only by
# codeflare-managed entrypoint scripts and system package tooling. No fix
# available in Debian bookworm (requires Archive::Tar 3.10).
CVE-2026-9538

# perl-modules-5.36 (Debian bookworm): heap buffer overflow in Perl_study_chunk
# (regcomp_study.c) when compiling regex with large quantified fixed substrings
# on 32-bit builds - mincount * length overflows SSize_t, undersizing SvGROW.
# Impact: NONE - container is 64-bit (x86_64), the overflow requires 32-bit
# SSize_t; additionally, no untrusted regex patterns are compiled. No fix
# available in Debian bookworm (requires Perl 5.44+).
CVE-2026-8376

# Go stdlib in rclone v1.73.5 (Go 1.25.x build): incomplete fix for URL
# escaping inside HTML <meta> tag content attribute - ASCII whitespace around
# '=' bypasses the escaper, leading to XSS. Impact: NONE - rclone does not
# serve HTML or render URLs into HTML templates; it performs outbound R2 bisync
# only. Fix: Go 1.25.10 / 1.26.3; rclone upstream has not rebuilt (pinned at
# v1.73.5 for bisync stability).
CVE-2026-39823

# Go stdlib in rclone v1.73.5 (Go 1.25.x build): ReverseProxy forwards query
# parameters hidden from Rewrite functions when GODEBUG urlmaxqueryparams limit
# is exceeded. Impact: NONE - rclone does not use httputil.ReverseProxy; it is
# an outbound-only CLI tool doing R2 bisync. Fix: Go 1.25.10 / 1.26.3.
CVE-2026-39825

# Go stdlib in rclone v1.73.5 (Go 1.25.x build): html/template incorrectly
# escapes <script> blocks when the type attribute is empty or contains ASCII
# whitespace. Impact: NONE - rclone does not use html/template or serve HTML;
# it is an outbound-only CLI tool doing R2 bisync. Fix: Go 1.25.10 / 1.26.3.
CVE-2026-39826

# python3-pip-whl 23.0.1+dfsg-1 (Debian bookworm): pip path traversal via a
# malicious entry point name during wheel installation. Impact: NONE - the
# container never pip-installs attacker-controlled wheels; python3/pip ships as
# part of the Debian toolchain and python is added only for ECC observe hooks
# (stdlib only, same exposure profile as the cpython entries above). No untrusted
# wheel is installed at runtime. No fixed version available in Debian bookworm.
CVE-2026-8643

# perl-modules-5.36 / libperl5.36 / perl / perl-base (Debian bookworm):
# IO::Compress arbitrary code execution via attacker-controlled output glob
# pattern. Impact: NONE - the container never calls IO::Compress with
# attacker-controlled glob arguments; perl is invoked only by
# codeflare-managed entrypoint scripts and system package tooling. Same
# exposure profile as the Archive::Tar perl entries above (CVE-2026-42496 /
# CVE-2026-42497 / CVE-2026-9538). No fixed version available in Debian bookworm.
CVE-2026-48962

# protobufjs / ws transitive deps in globally-installed npm agent CLIs
# (@openai/codex, opencode-ai, @github/copilot, @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent,
# consult-llm-mcp, browser-run-mcp, context-mode). Not in our own package.json
# (our host ws is already ^8.21.0); these are vendored-CLI transitives. Both are
# fixable upstream but the consuming CLIs have not rebuilt yet. Same "transitive
# dep of globally-installed npm CLIs, waiting for upstream" profile as the
# picomatch / glob / minimatch / node-tar entries above.
#
# CVE-2026-48712: protobufjs 7.5.9 — DoS via unbounded `Any` expansion during JSON
#   conversion. The CLIs decode protobuf only from trusted backends (gRPC/telemetry),
#   never attacker-controlled Any messages. Fix: 7.6.1 / 8.4.1.
CVE-2026-48712
# CVE-2026-48779: ws 8.20.1 — memory-exhaustion DoS from many tiny fragments/chunks.
#   The CLIs use ws as a client / local MCP transport in the single-tenant sandboxed
#   container, not a public server accepting untrusted peers. Fix: 7.5.11 / 8.21.0.
CVE-2026-48779

# cpython 3.11 (libpython3.11-minimal, libpython3.11-stdlib, python3.11,
# python3.11-minimal, python3.11-venv; 3.11.2-6+deb12u7, Debian bookworm): two
# new stdlib advisories. python3 is added for ECC observe hooks only (stdlib,
# run locally on tool-use events) — no network services and no untrusted input
# reach the stdlib parsers. Same package and exposure profile as the existing
# cpython 3.11 entries above (CVE-2026-7210, CVE-2026-6100, CVE-2025-69534, etc.).
# No fixed version available in Debian bookworm.
#
# CVE-2026-3644: incomplete control character validation in the stdlib.
CVE-2026-3644
# CVE-2026-4224: stack overflow parsing XML with a deeply nested DTD (XML DoS;
#                same no-untrusted-XML rationale as CVE-2026-7210 above).
CVE-2026-4224

# cpython 3.11 (libpython3.11-minimal, libpython3.11-stdlib, python3.11,
# python3.11-minimal, python3.11-venv; 3.11.2-6+deb12u7, Debian bookworm):
# tarfile module misinterprets a crafted tar header, allowing extraction
# outside the intended directory. Impact: NONE - python3 is added for ECC
# observe hooks only (stdlib, run locally on tool-use events); the container
# extracts no attacker-controlled tar archives via python tarfile. Same
# package and exposure profile as the existing cpython tarfile/XML entries
# above (CVE-2025-8194 tarfile infinite loop, CVE-2026-7210, CVE-2026-4224).
# No fixed version available in Debian bookworm.
CVE-2025-13462

# Go stdlib in rclone v1.73.x / lazygit v0.60.0 (Go 1.25.x / 1.26.x builds):
# DoS when decoding a maliciously-crafted MIME header containing many fields
# (net/textproto / mime). Impact: NONE - these are outbound-only CLI tools in
# the sandboxed container; they never parse untrusted MIME headers as a server.
# Same exposure profile as the other Go stdlib rclone/lazygit entries above
# (CVE-2026-42499 net/mail consumePhrase DoS, CVE-2025-61725). Fix: Go 1.25.11
# / 1.26.4; rclone and lazygit upstream have not rebuilt yet.
CVE-2026-42504

# perl 5.36 in the Debian bookworm base image (libperl5.36, perl, perl-base,
# perl-modules-5.36; 5.36.0-7+deb12u3): IO::Uncompress::Unzip before 2.220
# allows CPU exhaustion (DoS) when decompressing a crafted Zip stream. Impact:
# NONE - perl ships as a base-image/git tooling dependency; the container never
# feeds attacker-controlled Zip archives through perl's IO::Uncompress. Same
# base-image-dependency, no-untrusted-input exposure profile as the entries
# above. No fixed version available in Debian bookworm.
CVE-2026-48959

# Go stdlib crypto/x509 in lazygit v0.60.0 (Go 1.25.10) and silverbullet (Go 1.25.1):
# (*x509.Certificate).VerifyHostname mishandles hostname verification for certificates
# whose name fields were previously rejected, weakening the match decision. Impact: NONE -
# both are local CLI/UI tools in the sandboxed container that make outbound TLS only to
# trusted endpoints (lazygit: git-over-HTTPS to user remotes; silverbullet: serves the
# vault UI behind Cloudflare Access) and never make a trust decision on an
# attacker-controlled server certificate. Same Go 1.25.11 / 1.26.4 security batch as
# CVE-2026-42504 above; lazygit and silverbullet upstream have not rebuilt with the
# patched Go.
CVE-2026-27145

# sigstore 4.1.0 vendored inside the globally-installed npm CLI in the Node base
# image (usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/sigstore): CVE-2026-48815 -
# sigstore's certificateOIDs verification constraints are not fully enforced, so a
# crafted signing certificate could bypass an intended OID match during Sigstore
# bundle verification. Impact: NONE - npm only exercises sigstore at publish/audit
# time (npm publish provenance, npm audit signatures) against the trusted public
# registry; the container installs from trusted registries at build time and the
# deployed worker never runs npm to verify attacker-controlled Sigstore bundles at
# runtime. Fix: sigstore 4.1.1, which arrives when the pinned Node base image
# bundles a newer npm - not independently upgradable without bumping node/npm. Same
# "fixed upstream but a vendored CLI hasn't rebuilt" profile as the entries above.
CVE-2026-48815

# Go stdlib os.Root in gh (Go 1.26.4), lazygit v0.60.0 (Go 1.25.10), and
# silverbullet (Go 1.25.1): a symlink-following bug in os.Root lets filesystem
# operations meant to be confined to a root directory traverse outside it via a
# symlink, enabling directory traversal. Impact: NONE - all three are local,
# single-tenant CLI/UI tools in the sandboxed container operating on the user's
# OWN files (gh: user repos/config; lazygit: user git working trees; silverbullet:
# the user's Vault dir behind Cloudflare Access). There is no attacker-controlled
# symlink tree and no multi-tenant filesystem boundary for an os.Root confinement
# escape to cross. Fix: Go 1.25.12 / 1.26.5 / 1.27.0-rc.2; gh, lazygit, and
# silverbullet upstream have not rebuilt with the patched Go. Same vendored-binary
# exposure profile as CVE-2026-27145 above.
CVE-2026-39822

# ============================================================================
# OpenVSCode Server v1.109.5 (REQ-IDE-001, the per-session browser IDE): the
# pinned, vendored Gitpod VS Code web-server build added to the container image.
# All 22 HIGH/CRITICAL findings are reported ONLY under
# opt/openvscode-server/{extensions,node_modules}/ (baked into the pinned
# tarball; unfixable without rebuilding the vendored binary, and a newer
# OpenVSCode release ships the same built-in-extension CVEs).
#
# SCOPE NOTE: `undici`, `diff`, and `ini` also appear in codeflare's OWN trees
# (root, web-ui, and the baked-in pi agent tree under /opt/codeflare/pi-agent/
# npm), but every own-tree copy is already on a PATCHED version (undici
# 7.28.0/8.3.0, diff 8.0.4, ini 1.3.8) — which is why Trivy reports these IDs
# only under opt/openvscode-server. The plain .trivyignore suppresses by ID
# image-wide (no path scoping), so nothing is masked today; but if the
# pi/web-ui/root undici|diff|ini ever drift back into an affected range this
# allowlist would hide it — re-audit these three on ANY of those bumps, not only
# on an OPENVSCODE_VERSION bump.
#
# Impact: NONE across codeflare's trust boundary. OpenVSCode binds 127.0.0.1
# only, one instance per the user's OWN session container, reachable solely
# through the Worker's Cloudflare Access + tier + session-ownership auth chain.
# An AI coding agent DOES clone arbitrary repos, so untrusted workspace content
# (Gruntfiles, package.json, .ini, .hbs, WS peers) can reach some of these
# components — but any resulting code-execution/file-write is confined to the
# ephemeral, single-tenant container the session occupant already fully controls
# via their own shell, and none of these CVEs is a container-escape or
# cross-tenant primitive. Same vendored-binary, single-tenant-sandbox acceptance
# policy as CVE-2026-39822.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Built-in language extensions. The grammar / language-config extensions
# (handlebars, diff, ini, json) never invoke the flagged npm package at runtime
# (no Handlebars.compile(), no INI/JSON parser on that dep) -> vulnerable path
# unreachable. The grunt and npm extensions DO ship runtime detection JS, but
# only shell out to the workspace's own grunt/npm on the user's own in-container
# files (in-container-by-design, not a boundary crossing). Old CVEs inherent to
# every VS Code / OpenVSCode build.
#
# extensions/handlebars — prototype-pollution / RCE via template compilation:
CVE-2019-19919
CVE-2021-23369
CVE-2021-23383
CVE-2019-20920
GHSA-2cf5-4w76-r9qv
GHSA-g9r4-xpmj-mj65
GHSA-q2c6-c6pm-g3gh
GHSA-q42p-pg8m-cqh6
# extensions/diff — ReDoS in the diff grammar helper dep:
GHSA-h6ch-v84p-w6p9
# extensions/grunt — race-condition arbitrary file write / RCE via Gruntfile:
CVE-2020-7729
CVE-2022-1537
# extensions/npm — symlink/bin-field arbitrary file write + permission assignment:
CVE-2018-7408
CVE-2019-16775
CVE-2019-16776
CVE-2019-16777
# extensions/ini — prototype pollution via malicious INI file:
CVE-2020-7788
# extensions/json — command injection in trentm/json:
CVE-2020-7712
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server node_modules run inside the single-tenant session container only, behind
# the Worker auth boundary, on the user's own workspace.
#
# node_modules/shell-quote — arbitrary code execution via command injection:
CVE-2026-9277
# node_modules/undici — WebSocket DoS (unbounded memory / crafted frames); the
# IDE server uses undici as an outbound client, not an inbound parser for
# attacker-controlled requests:
CVE-2026-1526
CVE-2026-1528
CVE-2026-2229
# node_modules/lodash-es — arbitrary code execution via untrusted template imports:
CVE-2026-4800
